  //
  // Every amount in the snapshot is a decimal string exactly as the billing API
  // reported it, and it stays a string all the way to the page. Reading one into
  // a JavaScript number would quietly restate AWS's figure — 0.1 + 0.2 is not
  // 0.3 in binary floating point — and a report that rounds the bill is worse
  // than one that shows no bill at all.
  //
  // So arithmetic here is exact, on scaled integers: {u, e} means u / 10^e with
  // u a BigInt. Sums and comparisons align scales first and never round. The one
  // place a float appears is a bar width, which decides pixels, not figures.

  // DEC_RE is the grammar of internal/cost.ValidDecimal, kept in step by a
  // parity test. The lookahead says "and nothing after this" outright rather
  // than relying on "$", whose meaning depends on a flag: add "m" and "$" starts
  // matching at a line break, which would accept "1.00\n" here and see it
  // rejected by the collector.
